\begin{abstract}
The primitive Pythagorean triples carry two classical ternary tree structures: the
Barning--Hall--Berggren tree and the Price tree. In Euclid parameters $(m,n)$ both are
ternary trees on the \emph{same} vertex set
$\V=\{(m,n): 1\le n<m,\ \gcd(m,n)=1,\ m+n\ \text{odd}\}$ rooted at $(2,1)$; we call this
coincidence the \emph{interlock}. We prove the \emph{$N$-node identity}: for every
factorization $N=pq$ with $p,q$ odd, coprime and $1\le p<q$, the Fermat pair
$\bigl(\tfrac{p+q}{2},\tfrac{q-p}{2}\bigr)$ is a vertex of $\V$ whose odd leg equals $N$
exactly, and $(m,n)\mapsto(m-n,m+n)$ is a bijection from $\V$ onto the set of such
factorizations. Hence every odd semiprime has a unique address in each tree and
``factoring $N$ is finding the $N$-node'' is a theorem. We then prove three exact
separations of the two descents (determinants $\pm1$ versus $\pm2$, hence no conjugacy;
leg-swap symmetry for Berggren but not for Price; exactly two shared edges), and we close
the factoring question quantitatively: Price depth is size-driven ($m\le 2^{d+1}$) and its
level width already exceeds Fermat's entire scan, while Berggren depth is ratio-driven
($m\le(2d+3)n$), yielding the trade-off inequality $m\le 2s(2d+3)^2$ with $s$ Fermat's scan
length. Cheap Fermat scans force deep tree addresses: the two cost measures are inversely
coupled. Finally, the alternative hypotenuse embedding is obstructed: if $N$ has a prime
factor $p\equiv 3 \pmod 4$, no primitive triple has hypotenuse divisible by $N$.
\end{abstract}

